PLAIN WORDS (patch ignores this header; apply from the kit folder with:  patch -p1 < this file)
FIX 3: apply.<layer>.role_dials only worked for the soot layer (it multiplied two dials by hand). Now Kit.apply notes which dials the set varies by role
(dials.ROLE_DIAL_NAMES) and WallLayer.dial() multiplies exactly those by the object's value wk_<layer>_<dial>, for foot, runoff, wallcracks, wallgrowth, steps and paint alike.
Soot lists its two hand-made dials in role_handled, so its node graph is unchanged. A role value of 0 reads as 'not named' (= 1), so 'off' is written as a hair above 0
(MIN_ROLE_MULT 0.002).
TESTED: all 11 layer node groups built for the street set have identical node and link counts and node kinds before and after; with role_dials in the courtyard set,
foot gains 2 Attribute nodes (one per varied dial) and wallgrowth 3, nothing else changes; the per-object values land on the right objects (plaster lichen 0.002, stone 2.5).
NOT TESTED: a picture showing lichen off plaster and on stone.

--- a/apply/dials.py
+++ b/apply/dials.py
@@ -113,6 +113,22 @@
     return result
 
 
+# The dials the CURRENT set varies by role, per layer ({layer: {dial, ...}}), noted by Kit.apply just before the layers are built. A walls layer multiplies exactly these dials by the
+# object's role value (WallLayer.dial); a dial the set does not vary costs no node. Plain Python.
+ROLE_DIAL_NAMES = {}
+MIN_ROLE_MULT = 0.002      # the smallest role multiplier written: an object value of 0 reads as "the set names none" (1), so "off" is written as a hair above 0
+
+
+def note_role_dials(settings):
+    """Fills ROLE_DIAL_NAMES from the set's apply.<layer>.role_dials (the union over the roles). Call it before building a layer's node group."""
+    ROLE_DIAL_NAMES.clear()
+    for layer in settings.apply:
+        names = {dial for table in role_multipliers(settings, layer).values() for dial in table}
+        if names:
+            ROLE_DIAL_NAMES[layer] = names
+    return ROLE_DIAL_NAMES
+
+
 def object_attribute_name(layer, dial):
     return f'wk_{layer}_{dial}'
 
